.Dd October 1, 2026
.Dt NETLINK 7
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm netlink
.Nd the kernel's answers about interfaces, addresses, routes and sockets
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.In linux/netlink.h
.In linux/rtnetlink.h
.Ft int
.Fn socket "AF_NETLINK" "SOCK_RAW" "NETLINK_ROUTE"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An
.Dv AF_NETLINK
socket of protocol
.Dv NETLINK_ROUTE
(0) is how a program asks the kernel what network interfaces, IPv4
addresses and routes it has, as on Linux.
.Fa type
is
.Dv SOCK_RAW
or
.Dv SOCK_DGRAM ,
which are the same socket, and may carry
.Dv SOCK_CLOEXEC
and
.Dv SOCK_NONBLOCK .
Any user may open one.
.Pp
A socket of protocol
.Dv NETLINK_SOCK_DIAG
(4) answers the dump request
.Dv SOCK_DIAG_BY_FAMILY
(20), with which
.Xr ss 8
lists sockets.
For
.Dv AF_INET
and the protocol 6, 17 or 255 it gives the TCP, UDP or raw sockets in the
states the request's mask has, each a
.Vt struct inet_diag_msg
with the ports and addresses, the queues, the owner and the inode, a
listener's queues being the connections waiting to be accepted and the most
it holds, and a
raw socket's protocol in an
.Dv INET_DIAG_PROTOCOL
attribute; the states are TCP's numbers, a datagram socket being 1 when
connected and 7 otherwise, and a listener's local address is 0.0.0.0.
For
.Dv AF_UNIX
it gives the local sockets with a descriptor open, each a
.Vt struct unix_diag_msg
with the name, the peer's inode, the two queues and the owner when the
request's
.Fa udiag_show
asks for them.
For
.Dv AF_INET6
the dump is empty.
Another family or protocol is answered with
.Er ENOENT ,
as Linux answers when no module handles it, and a request without
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP
with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
A filter's bytecode in the request is not run; the timers, the extensions
.Fa idiag_ext
asks for, and the memory and TCP information are not given.
.Pp
A socket of protocol
.Dv NETLINK_KOBJECT_UEVENT
(15), which udev's monitors open to hear of devices that come and go, is
made, bound and polled like one of
.Dv NETLINK_ROUTE
and hears nothing, since the kernel sends no uevents; what is sent on it
is dropped.
Another protocol is
.Er EPROTONOSUPPORT .
.Pp
A request is a netlink message, a
.Vt struct nlmsghdr
and its body, sent to the kernel with
.Xr send 2 ,
.Xr sendto 2
or
.Xr sendmsg 2 ;
the address of a
.Xr sendto 2
is not read, since the kernel is the only destination.
Several messages may be sent in one call; they end at one shorter than a
header or longer than the bytes left, and what follows is ignored, as
Linux ignores it, so a request may be sent in a buffer longer than itself,
as iproute2's
.Xr ip 8
sends one.
The answers are queued on the socket as datagrams of messages, each at
most 4096 bytes, which
.Xr recv 2
reads one at a time; a receive reports the kernel's address, a
.Vt struct sockaddr_nl
with port id 0.
.Xr recvmsg 2
with
.Dv MSG_PEEK
and
.Dv MSG_TRUNC
gives the length of the datagram waiting and leaves it, which is how
.Xr ip 8
sizes its buffer.
Each answer repeats the request's sequence number and the socket's port
id.
.Pp
The requests answered are:
.Bl -tag -width "RTM_GETQDISC"
.It Dv RTM_GETLINK
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
an
.Dv RTM_NEWLINK
message for each interface, with its index, its flags
.Dv ( IFF_UP ,
.Dv IFF_RUNNING
and
.Dv IFF_LOWER_UP
while it is up, and
.Dv IFF_BROADCAST
and
.Dv IFF_MULTICAST ) ,
and the attributes
.Dv IFLA_IFNAME ,
.Dv IFLA_ADDRESS ,
.Dv IFLA_BROADCAST ,
.Dv IFLA_MTU ,
.Dv IFLA_OPERSTATE ,
and
.Dv IFLA_STATS
with the packets, bytes and drops each way and the receive errors, which
.Ql ip -s link
prints.
Without it, the one message of the interface whose index the
.Vt struct ifinfomsg
names, or with an index of 0, whose name an
.Dv IFLA_IFNAME
attribute holds, with or without its terminating NUL, which is how
.Ql ip link show eth0
asks; or an
.Dv NLMSG_ERROR
of
.Er ENODEV .
.It Dv RTM_GETADDR
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
an
.Dv RTM_NEWADDR
message for each interface with an IPv4 address: its prefix length and
the attributes
.Dv IFA_ADDRESS ,
.Dv IFA_LOCAL ,
.Dv IFA_BROADCAST
and
.Dv IFA_LABEL .
.It Dv RTM_GETROUTE
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
an
.Dv RTM_NEWROUTE
message for each interface's own subnet, reached on the link, and one for
its default route through its gateway when it has one, in the main table.
Without
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
as
.Ql ip route get
asks, one
.Dv RTM_NEWROUTE
message for the address of the request's
.Dv RTA_DST :
a local route in the local table for an address an interface holds, and
otherwise the unicast route the stack takes, with its interface, source
address and gateway, marked
.Dv RTM_F_CLONED
as Linux marks a route it looked up; an address nothing reaches is
.Er ENETUNREACH .
.It Dv RTM_GETRULE
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
the three rules every Linux machine starts with, which are what a route
lookup here amounts to: the local table at priority 0, the main table at
32766 and the default table at 32767.
.It Dv RTM_GETQDISC
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
a
.Ql noqueue
discipline at the root of each interface, since a frame goes to the card
when it is sent, with no queue before it.
.It Dv RTM_GETNEIGH
With
.Dv NLM_F_DUMP ,
an
.Dv RTM_NEWNEIGH
message for each mapping of the ARP cache, with the attributes
.Dv NDA_DST
and
.Dv NDA_LLADDR ,
in the state
.Dv NUD_REACHABLE ,
since the cache keeps no age; only those of one interface when the
.Vt struct ndmsg
or an
.Dv NDA_IFINDEX
attribute names it, and none for a family other than IPv4 or any, or for
the proxy table, which this kernel does not have.
.El
.Pp
A dump ends with an
.Dv NLMSG_DONE
message, and every message of a dump has
.Dv NLM_F_MULTI .
A request of another type, or one that would change the configuration,
such as
.Dv RTM_NEWADDR ,
is answered with an
.Dv NLMSG_ERROR
of
.Er EOPNOTSUPP :
the interface requests of
.Xr ioctl 2
are how a program configures this machine.
.Pp
.Xr bind 2
takes a
.Vt struct sockaddr_nl
of
.Dv AF_NETLINK :
a port id of 0 has the kernel pick one, the process's ID for its first
socket and a number above 2^31 for the rest, and another taken by a
socket already is
.Er EADDRINUSE .
The multicast groups are kept, and nothing is sent to them: the kernel
sends no notifications.
A socket that sends before it is bound, or whose name
.Xr getsockname 2
asks for, is bound then, as on Linux.
.Xr connect 2
succeeds and changes nothing, and
.Xr getpeername 2
reports the kernel.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr ioctl 2 ,
.Xr recv 2 ,
.Xr send 2 ,
.Xr socket 2
